Since 2023, Apple's newer iPhones have had an 80 percent charge limit for those who want to extend battery lifespan. Charging to 80 percent instead of 100 percent is supposed to put less stress on the battery, improving longevity.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/08/iOS-26-Battery-Glass-Feature.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1016307" /><br/>
For the last three years, I've used that setting on my iPhone. I started <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2024/09/24/iphone-80-percent-charging-test/">with the iPhone 15 Pro Max</a>, then did <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/09/24/iphone-16-pro-max-charge-limit-test/">the iPhone 16 Pro Max</a>, and most recently, the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-17/">iPhone 17</a> Pro Max.<br/>
<br/>
After 12 months at an 80 percent maximum charge, <strong>my iPhone 17 Pro Max is at 97 percent maximum capacity</strong> with a cycle count of 277.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/iphone-17-pro-max-battery-1-year.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1125" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1069063" /><br/>
Last year, my <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-16/">iPhone 16</a> Pro Max was at 94 percent capacity after 12 months, and in 2024, my iPhone 15 Pro Max was also at 94 percent capacity.<br/>
<br/>
During the year I tested the &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro Max, I tried not to let the battery dip below 20 percent. This year, I did the same thing, but I also tried to do more charging over USB-C than Qi2 or <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/magsafe/">MagSafe</a>. I'm not sure if that helped, but 97 percent capacity is the best result I've had yet with the charge limit turned on.<br/>
<br/>
I don't know how my battery result compares to others, but if you've kept yours at 100 percent, you can compare your capacity to mine to see if there's any real difference.<br/>
<br/>
Apple transitioned to aluminum and added vapor chamber cooling with the &zwnj;iPhone 17&zwnj; Pro Max, and I did think my iPhone spent less time uncomfortably warm than the &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro Max. It may be that the vapor chamber cooling made the biggest difference and limiting wireless charging didn't matter.<br/>
<br/>
I don't use the iPhone 15 Pro Max or the &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro Max as my main iPhones anymore, but I do use them daily as test devices. They still have the 80 percent limit turned on, so I can share some longer-term battery results.<br/>
<ul><br/>
 	<li>iPhone 15 Pro Max (36 months) - 88%, 369 cycles</li><br/>
 	<li>&zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro Max (24 months) - 93%, 343 cycles</li><br/>
 	<li>&zwnj;iPhone 17&zwnj; Pro Max (12 months) - 97%, 277 cycles</li><br/>
</ul><br/>
Last year, my &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro Max hit 299 cycles by September 24. This year, I'm only at 277 cycles. It's nearly two weeks later than I usually report results too, so the larger battery meant less charging.<br/>
<br/>
The &zwnj;iPhone 17&zwnj; Pro Max has a bigger battery and faster charging than the &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro Max, so an 80 percent charge limit wasn't as difficult to live with as it was in the past. I rarely ran out of battery, though I did still bring a battery pack with me most places. My favorite battery packs right now are the ones with built-in USB-C cables, especially those that can attach to my iPhone magnetically but charge it over USB-C. It's faster charging that's still convenient. I also continue to be a fan of the super-slim battery packs that add little bulk and weight to an iPhone.<br/>
<br/>
For the iPhone 15 Pro Max and &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro Max, I didn't think sacrificing a fifth of my battery was worth the capacity I ended up with, and that's still mostly the case. I'm guessing my 97 percent capacity isn't too far off from the battery health readings on iPhones that didn't have the charge limit turned on.<br/>
<br/>
As for older devices, my two-year-old &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro Max only lost 1 percent capacity from 2025 to 2026, and my iPhone 15 Pro Max didn't lose any capacity at all during the year. Both phones had fewer battery cycles (44 and 17, respectively), but they're holding capacity even after sitting dead for short periods of time throughout the year.<br/>
<br/>
The most hands-off way to manage battery health is to use Optimized Battery Charging, since it minimizes time spent at 100 percent battery while still charging to full. Setting an 80 percent limit and then turning it off on days you need 100 percent is also a good compromise that could save you battery capacity over time. Apple has 90 and 95 percent options too, which might be a better choice.<br/>
<br/>
My <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-18-pro/">iPhone 18 Pro</a> Max is already set to 80 percent, so I'll check in again in September 2027. I'm going to stick with USB-C charging when I can, and I'm curious how the even bigger battery will affect longevity and what the 80 percent limit is like day to day.<br/>
